      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>Statutory Minimum Floor Protection for HRA</h2>
        <p>
          In major metropolitan cities like Mumbai, Bengaluru, and New Delhi, residential rentals operate with high baseline costs. If HRA were evaluated strictly as a percentage of entry-level basic pay without minimum floor protections, lower-level staff would receive insufficient rental allowances.
        </p>
        <p>
          To safeguard junior personnel, the Ministry of Finance enforces <strong>statutory minimum floor guarantees</strong> below which HRA cannot fall, regardless of basic pay:
        </p>
        <div class="formula-box">
          <p><strong>7th CPC Floor Limits:</strong> Class X = ₹5,400/month | Class Y = ₹3,600/month | Class Z = ₹1,800/month</p>
          <p><strong>8th CPC Projected Floor Limits:</strong> Class X = ₹11,080/month | Class Y = ₹7,390/month | Class Z = ₹3,690/month</p>
        </div>
        <p>
          <em>Operational Example:</em> Under the 8th CPC, even if an entry-level operational employee at Pay Level 1 (with an entry basic of ₹41,040) is posted in a Class X city where 27% HRA equals ₹11,081, the statutory floor ensures that under no circumstances can their monthly rental allocation drop below the guaranteed minimum threshold.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="editorial-chapter">
        <h2>The 0% DA Reset: Why Base-Rebuilding Delivers Higher Cash Accretion</h2>
        <p>
          When employees review pay projections under the 8th Pay Commission, a common concern is the reset of Dearness Allowance to 0%: <em>"Does resetting DA to 0% mean an initial loss of inflation protection?"</em>
        </p>
        <p>
          The answer lies in the mathematical leverage of <strong>base-rebuilding</strong>. Dearness Allowance at 53% on a smaller, decade-old basic pay delivers far less cash value than even a small DA percentage applied to a modern, revised basic pay.
        </p>
        <div class="callout-box">
          <h4>Comparing a 3% DA Hike Before and After Revision (Level 6):</h4>
          <p>
            <strong>Under 7th CPC (Basic ₹35,400):</strong> A 3% DA hike yielded: 0.03 &times; ₹35,400 = <strong>₹1,062 per month</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Under 8th CPC (Basic ₹80,712):</strong> That same 3% DA hike yields: 0.03 &times; ₹80,712 = <strong>₹2,421 per month</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
